From b3a5f38f51cae10a9f6af3ba1c31f9f3c8685844 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 20:23:47 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] fix(sim): wire laws judge each skim pass at its own kerf
 (#538 over #534)

Main's wire verify measured every move against the rough condition's
kerf (0.125 + 0.04 = 0.165 mm), so skim 1's path at its own offset
0.145 (0.125 + 0.02) read as a 0.0200 mm gouge at move 8 although its
real kerf edge lies on the profile. WireJob now carries each skim's
own kerf offset; the path is split at each return to the threading
point (the rule the post uses) and each move is judged at its own
pass's half-width - never narrower than that pass's real kerf. The
threading law takes the widest pass; the slug law sweeps each pass at
its own offset. A path that does not return once per pass, or an open
run with skims, is refused by name (also at the door in check_passes).

Refs #538

